.TH FAIRSEM 1 "2026-08-30" "fairsem 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fairsem \- fair, observable admission semaphore for local Linux commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fairsem run
[\fB--name\fR NAME] [\fB--slots\fR N] [\fB--timeout\fR SECONDS]
[\fB--poll\fR SECONDS] [\fB--best-effort\fR] \fB--\fR COMMAND [ARG...]
.br
.B fairsem status
[\fB--name\fR NAME] [\fB--json\fR]
.br
.B fairsem repair
[\fB--name\fR NAME]
.SH DESCRIPTION
FairSem gives independent processes owned by one user a named, local admission
queue. It assigns tickets under an exclusive kernel lock and admits the oldest
eligible live waiters without exceeding the configured slot count.
.PP
Version 0.1 supports Linux with /proc and Python 3.10 or newer. FairSem is not a
distributed lock, scheduler, resource limiter, or security boundary between
processes sharing a Unix account.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --name NAME
Semaphore name. Default: default. Names use letters, digits, dot, underscore,
and hyphen; the first character must be alphanumeric.
.TP
.B --slots N
Positive slot count. Default: 1. A conflicting count is rejected while a named
semaphore is active and may replace its configuration when idle.
.TP
.B --timeout SECONDS
Maximum monotonic time to wait for the state lock and capacity. Exit 75 without
launching on expiry.
.TP
.B --poll SECONDS
Admission polling interval. Default: 0.05.
.TP
.B --best-effort
If secure initial state setup/locking is unavailable, print a warning and run
without admission. Corrupt state and configuration conflicts remain errors.
.TP
.B --json
Emit the stable status JSON schema instead of human text.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FAIRSEM_STATE_DIR
Owner-only state root. Default: /tmp/fairsem-$UID. Must be a real directory
owned by the effective user with mode 0700.
.TP
.B FAIRSEM_HELD_NAMES
Internal colon-separated nesting guard passed to child commands. Users should
not set it directly.
.SH FAIRNESS
With one slot, admission is FIFO among live waiters. With N slots, only the
oldest available-capacity group is eligible, allowing at most N-1 scheduling
overtakes. Priorities are intentionally absent in 0.1.
.SH SIGNALS AND CRASHES
INT, TERM, and HUP are forwarded to the command's new process group. A command
remains the recorded holder if its wrapper is killed, and dead/zombie records
are removed by the next operation using boot ID, PID, and /proc start-time
identity.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Command status is preserved. Pre-launch tool codes are: 64 usage, 65 config,
69 unsupported platform, 70 corrupt state, 73 unsafe/unavailable state or lock,
75 timeout, and 126 cannot execute. Signal exits use 128 plus signal.
.SH SECURITY
Status and owner-readable state contain command arguments. Do not put secrets
on command lines. Default behavior fails closed when state cannot be trusted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/fairsem-$UID
Default state root.
.SH SEE ALSO
flock(2), proc(5)
.SH BUGS
Report issues at https://github.com/korovin-aa97/fairsem/issues
